fuxi: 0134 -- a debug shell on the USB command channel

The console streams one way and clips its own first minute; the command channel
(0110) could only stat, cpu, echo and restart.  While this kernel runs there is no
adb, no shell and no dmesg, so every question about a stuck boot -- who is
spinning, what a process is waiting on, what a file actually says -- had to be
answered by adding a printf and rebooting.  That is the wrong loop to be in when
the thing being debugged is the boot itself.

The phone side of the existing socket thread now dispatches to a small kernel
shell, so the answers keep coming while launchd is wedged:

  help                     this text
  uptime                   seconds since boot
  ps [n]                   processes by CPU time, heaviest first
  cat <path> [bytes]       read a file through the VFS (default 4096, max 12288)
  kill <pid> [sig]         signal a process (default 15 = SIGTERM)
  smp <pid|name>           sample that process's user stacks (to the console)
  stub <name[,name...]>    stop those binaries at exec; '-' clears

Replies are chunked into 1 KiB datagrams and always end with the marker
"[[fuxi-shell-end]]", so a client reads until it sees it.  "stub" exists because
the boot restarts fairplayd.H2 and bluetoothd every few seconds (no baseband, no
SEP): 0133 could only set that deny list from the boot arguments, and testing
whether the respawn loop matters should not cost a reboot.  The list moved into a
global, written by the shell and read on every exec; the boot argument still
seeds it.

No new locking: the shell runs on the one command thread, proc_find/proc_iterate
give the references it holds, and a torn read of the stub list at worst stubs one
wrong process, which the next "exec %s stubbed" line shows.

Harness: applied=133 skipped=0.  fuxi_shell.c, fuxi_usb.c and kern_exec.c
compile against the CI tree's headers (tools/xnu-cc.py, clang 18 local).

